<h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-3 leading-snug font-display"><strong>11. Lion\'s Point</strong></h3>
<figure class="my-4"><img src="images/ChatGPT-Image-Jul-4-2025-07_55_03-PM.webp" alt="Lions Point Lonavala" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md"></figure>
<p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">Located midway between Bhushi Dam and the luxurious Aamby Valley, Lion\'s Point offers some of the most dramatic and rugged views in Lonavala. The steep cliff drops reveal a deep, plunging valley adorned with cascading waterfalls and dense forests. Because of the frequent heavy winds and the spectacular hues of the setting sun, it is widely regarded as one of the most incredibly scenic and <strong>romantic places in Lonavala</strong>.</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 mb-5">
    <li><strong>Things to do:</strong> Sunset photography, enjoying evening snacks like roasted corn (bhutta), and night drives.</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to visit:</strong> Late afternoons and evenings, especially during the monsoon and winter.</li>
    <li><strong>How to reach:</strong> About 12 kilometers from Lonavala station; renting a cab or driving your own vehicle is best.</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-3 leading-snug font-display"><strong>12. Bhaja Caves</strong></h3>
<figure class="my-4"><img src="images/ChatGPT-Image-Jul-4-2025-09_36_49-PM-1024x683.webp" alt="Bhaja Caves" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md"></figure>
<p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">A sister site to the Karla Caves, the Bhaja Caves are a group of 22 rock-cut caves dating back to the 2nd century BC. While they share architectural similarities with Karla, Bhaja is often less crowded and offers a deeply serene, mystical vibe. The caves feature a cluster of stunning stupas that were erected to shelter the relics of prominent Buddhist monks. During the monsoon, a prominent waterfall near the caves provides an added attraction.</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 mb-5">
    <li><strong>Things to do:</strong> Explore the intricately carved viharas and chaityas, and bathe in the nearby seasonal waterfall during the rains.</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to visit:</strong> Monsoons for the waterfalls, or winters for exploring the architecture without the heat.</li>
    <li><strong>How to reach:</strong> Accessible via Malavli railway station, which is just a short trek or auto ride away from the caves.</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-3 leading-snug font-display"><strong>13. Visapur Fort</strong></h3>
<figure class="my-4"><img src="images/Trek-Stay.jpeg" alt="Visapur Fort" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md"></figure>
<p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">Sitting slightly higher than its twin Lohagad Fort, Visapur Fort offers a far more challenging and adventurous trek. The fort\'s perimeter walls are largely intact and stretch across a massive plateau. The real highlight of the Visapur trek comes during the heavy rains when the rocky steps leading to the fort turn into cascading waterfalls, requiring trekkers to climb against the flow of water. This unique experience makes it a favorite for adventurous trekkers looking for thrilling <strong>places to visit in Lonavala</strong>.</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 mb-5">
    <li><strong>Things to do:</strong> Waterfall trekking, exploring the Peshwa Palace ruins, and spotting ancient cannons.</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to visit:</strong> The peak of the monsoon season for the waterfall trek.</li>
    <li><strong>How to reach:</strong> Start the trek from Malavli village or Bhaje village near Lonavala.</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-3 leading-snug font-display"><strong>14. Tikona Fort (Vitandgad)</strong></h3>
<figure class="my-4"><img src="images/Gemini_Generated_Image_euvpxieuvpxieuv.webp" alt="Tikona Fort" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md"></figure>
<p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">Easily recognizable by its distinct triangular (Tikona) shape, Vitandgad is a dominant hill fort in the Maval region. It provides an excellent, moderately easy trekking destination. The fort boasts large doors, a temple of \'Trimbakeshwar Mahadev\', water tanks (seven water tanks), and Satvahan caves. Upon reaching the summit, trekkers are greeted with stunning, uninterrupted views of the majestic Pawna Lake and the surrounding Sahyadri mountains.</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 mb-5">
    <li><strong>Things to do:</strong> Trekking, exploring the Trimbakeshwar temple, and enjoying panoramic views of Pawna Lake.</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to visit:</strong> Winter and early summer (October to March).</li>
    <li><strong>How to reach:</strong> The base village, Tikona Peth, is located about 25 kilometers from Lonavala.</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-3 leading-snug font-display"><strong>15. Duke\'s Nose (Nagphani)</strong></h3>
<figure class="my-4"><img src="images/Dukes-nose.webp" alt="Dukes Nose Lonavala" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md"></figure>
<p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">Named after the Duke of Wellington due to its prominent shape resembling his pointed nose, Duke\'s Nose (locally known as Nagphani or cobra\'s hood) is a famous cliff in Khandala. It is highly revered among rock climbers and adventure seekers due to its steep, rocky terrain. The trek to the top is rewarding, offering sweeping, dramatic views of the Khandala Ghat, making it a top pick for <strong>sightseeing in Lonavala</strong>. If you love discovering lesser-known spots, check out our guide on <a href="/hidden-places-in-lonavala.php" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline">hidden places in Lonavala</a>.</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-700 mb-5">
    <li><strong>Things to do:</strong> Rock climbing, rappelling, valley crossing, and sunset viewing.</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to visit:</strong> Winters are best for rock climbing, while monsoons offer the best scenic beauty.</li>
    <li><strong>How to reach:</strong> The trek can be started from Khandala station or Kurvande village.</li>
</ul>

